# @ManyToOne Refactor Reference Pattern

## Goal
Replace loose `Uuid $xxxId` columns with proper `@ManyToOne` entity relations so Doctrine knows about the 26 FK constraints and stops reporting them as drift.

## Established Pattern (from LyricsEntity — already done)

### Entity changes
```php
// BEFORE:
use App\Shared\Domain\Model\Uuid;

#[ORM\Column(type: 'uuid')]
private Uuid $songId;

// constructor: public function __construct(Uuid $id, Uuid $songId, ...)
// accessor:    public function getSongId(): Uuid { return $this->songId; }

// AFTER:
use App\Catalog\Infrastructure\Doctrine\Entity\SongEntity;

#[ORM\ManyToOne(targetEntity: SongEntity::class)]
#[ORM\JoinColumn(name: 'song_id', referencedColumnName: 'id', nullable: false, onDelete: 'CASCADE')]
private SongEntity $song;

// constructor: public function __construct(Uuid $id, SongEntity $song, ...)
// accessor:    public function getSongId(): Uuid { return $this->song->getId(); }
```

### Repository changes
```php
// BEFORE:
new LyricsEntity(id: $id, songId: $uuid, ...);
findOneBy(['songId' => $uuid]);

// AFTER:
use App\Catalog\Infrastructure\Doctrine\Entity\SongEntity;

new LyricsEntity(id: $id, song: $this->entityManager->getReference(SongEntity::class, $uuid), ...);
findOneBy(['song' => $uuid]);
```

### Index handling
- If the FK column already has a `#[ORM\UniqueConstraint]` covering it, DO NOT add a separate `#[ORM\Index]` — Doctrine drops it as redundant.
- If there is NO unique constraint on the column, add `#[ORM\Index(name: '<existing_custom_name>', columns: ['<col>'])]` to preserve the DB's existing index name.
- Doctrine will rename the FK constraint to a hashed `FK_<hash>` name. This is expected and acceptable — the migration will handle the rename (drop old FK + create new).

## Target entities & FK specs

See the per-task assignment for exact FK column, constraint name, target entity, and onDelete behavior.

